window.docContents[248].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Compactness-and-the-direct-method":{"contents":"Formalized: relative weak compactness for bounded Hilbert-space families, weakly\nclosed sets cut out by arbitrary families of continuous linear constraints,\npreservation of those constraints under weak limits, and their pointwise\nlifting to almost-everywhere constraints on Bochner  trajectories.\nThe lifted constraint spaces are weakly closed, survive weak  limits,\nand preserve weak compactness when imposed on bounded weakly closed families.\nContinuous linear functionals that vanish on the kernel of a closed-range\noperator factor continuously through its range and have a unique\nrange-valued Hilbert multiplier; this is the abstract pressure-recovery step\nfor a divergence operator.\nComponentwise weak convergence in two Hilbert spaces also preserves every\ncommon bound on a nonnegative weighted sum of squared norms, which is the\nkinetic-plus-dissipation limit passage used in energy-class constructions.\nThe formalized layer also includes weak lower semicontinuity of convex\nfunctionals, coercive direct-method theorems, Sobolev integral applications,\nspatial Rellich–Kondrachov compactness, and the weak Bochner compactness and\nLions–Magenes machinery used by the Galerkin chapters.\nFor compact dense Gelfand inclusions of real Hilbert spaces on a fixed\nfinite interval , the abstract Aubin–Lions theorem is formalized for\nLions–Magenes trajectories: uniform  bounds and\n bounds on their weak time derivatives imply relative\ncompactness in .\nFor Hölder-related exponents , strong  and\n convergence passes continuous bilinear pointwise operations to strong\n convergence. In particular, quadratic strong  convergence yields strong\n convergence. Weak dual trajectories can also be paired with strongly\nconvergent states inside the time integral. Continuous trilinear forms have\nabstract skew-symmetry lemmas for cubic energy cancellation and quadratic\ndifference estimates, a bounded-set Lipschitz estimate for the induced\ndual-valued quadratic map, and a strong–strong–weak limit theorem for\nbounded weak test families. Quadratic Gelfand weak equations satisfy both\na state-tested energy identity and an exact difference equation. At\nthe Galerkin level, skew cancellation transfers the dimension-independent\npivot and integrated coercive estimates from the linear equation to the\nquadratic equation. Finite-partition ODE solutions can be\ncontinued inside a time-dependent family of invariant sets, including\nmoving energy envelopes. The quadratic mass-inverted Galerkin vector field\nhas explicit growth and bounded-set Lipschitz estimates. Combining its local\nPicard theorem, finite-dimensional pivot norm equivalence, coercivity, and\nskew cancellation gives global Galerkin existence on every finite interval.\nThe argument uses no Navier–Stokes structure. A sign-unrestricted differential Grönwall\ntheorem and its dissipative corollary provide a complementary scalar route:\nif , , and , then a\ndifferentiable trajectory satisfying  on\n satisfies  throughout that interval.\n\nPartial: Aubin–Lions compactness covers Hilbert Gelfand triples with\n time integrability of both the trajectories and their weak time\nderivatives. Around it the evolution layer has coordinated common-filter weak\nlimits, identification of weak time derivatives, and the basic nonlinear limit\npassages that use its strong convergence.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Foundational Coverage and Open Problems","header":"3.5.1.5. Compactness and the direct method","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Compactness-and-the-direct-method"},"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Coordinate-derivatives-and-vector-operators--Conventions":{"contents":"One convention matters throughout the book: fderiv, and hence each operator\ndefined from it, is a total Lean function. At a point where the field is not\ndifferentiable it still has a formal value, normally zero. The definitions\ntherefore describe operator expressions, while theorems that interpret\nthem analytically carry differentiability or  hypotheses explicitly.\nThis separation lets the same operators appear in classical equations,\nsymbolic residuals, and weak-form bridges without storing regularity inside\nevery expression.\n\nSeveral locality statements refer to the germ of a function at .\nThis means its values on some neighborhood of , with functions identified\nwhen they agree on a possibly smaller neighborhood. A derivative can depend\non these nearby values without depending on the function far away.\n\n\n\nFor the th Euclidean basis vector , the coordinate derivative isThe implementation partialDeriv i f x evaluates Mathlib's total Fréchet\nderivative on . It is therefore a total expression even when  is\nnot differentiable, and denotes the classical partial derivative only under\nthe corresponding differentiability hypothesis.\n\nHigher derivatives are iterated coordinate derivatives, indexed by a finite word\nof coordinates rather than by a multi-index. Words keep the order of application\nexplicit, which matters until enough regularity is available to commute\nderivatives.\n\nA derivative word  and a field\n, where  is any real normed vector space, determine\nthe recursionConsequently,The head of the Lean list is the outermost derivative, while the final list\nentry is the derivative applied to  first.The construction does not require a scalar codomain. Every recursive stage\nstill maps  to the same normed space , so coordinate\ndifferentiation can be iterated for vector-, matrix-, or function-space-valued\nfields without introducing a second definition. Statements that interpret\nthe totalized expression analytically supply the regularity needed at every\niteration.\n\nThe recursion peels off the head of the word, so the first check concerns the\nother end.\n\nAppending a coordinate to the right of a derivative word means that this new\ncoordinate derivative acts on the original field first:This theorem pins down the list-order convention. It holds for fields with\narbitrary real normed-space codomain and is an identity between totalized\nderivative expressions, with no smoothness assumption.\n\nInduct on the list . For the empty list, both sides reduce to\n. For , unfold one recursive layer on each\nside. Both expressions become  applied to the induction\nhypothesis for , so they agree definitionally after rewriting.\n\nRepeating a single coordinate connects the word calculus with Mathlib's\nmultilinear iterated Fréchet derivative. This identification is the route by\nwhich symmetry and locality results for iteratedFDeriv are transferred to\ncoordinate derivatives.\n\nLet  denote coordinate differentiation in direction . If\n is  at , thenHere the right side is Mathlib's -linear iterated Fréchet derivative\nevaluated on  copies of the same coordinate vector. The codomain  is\nan arbitrary real normed vector space. The theorem is local: ContDiffAt at\n suffices, and no global smoothness is required.\n\nInduct on . At order zero both sides are . At the successor step,\napply the induction hypothesis to the first coordinate derivative. Evaluation of a\ncontinuous linear map at  is itself a continuous linear map, so it can\nbe moved through the iterated Fréchet derivative. Mathlib's successor formula\nfor iteratedFDeriv then identifies the result with the next multilinear\nderivative evaluated on one additional copy of .\n\nEach coordinate derivative costs one order of differentiability. This\nbookkeeping is needed whenever a derivative of a derivative must still be\ndifferentiated, for instance in the block-commutation law.\n\nCoordinate differentiation uses exactly the expected finite regularity\nbudget. If  is  at , thenThe statement is vector-valued and point-local. Setting  says that\n is  at , while larger  keeps smoothness in reserve\nfor later chain or product rules.\n\nInduct on . The zero case is the original regularity hypothesis. For a\nsuccessor, view one coordinate derivative as evaluation of fderiv on the\nconstant vector . Taking fderiv lowers the differentiability order by\none, and continuous-linear-map evaluation preserves the remaining order.\nApply the induction hypothesis to the resulting field.\n\nTotalized coordinate derivatives depend only on the germ of the field.\n\nRepeated coordinate derivatives are local operators. Suppose\n agree on a neighborhood of .* The repeated derivatives  and  agree on a neighborhood\n  of , and in particular .* The same holds after a block of  derivatives in coordinate \n  followed by a block of  derivatives in coordinate .No differentiability hypothesis enters, because the coordinate derivatives\nare those of the totalized Fréchet derivative.\n\nEquality of  and  near  gives equality of their Fréchet derivatives\nnear , hence of each coordinate derivative. Induction on the number of\nderivatives gives the first claim, and applying it to the -block and then\nto the -block gives the second.\n\nThe same first derivative also has a one-dimensional characterization.\nDifferentiability at  is needed, since the totalized fderiv and the\none-variable deriv need not agree otherwise.\n\nIf  is differentiable at , the coordinate derivative is the ordinary\nderivative along the th coordinate line:This connects the Fréchet definition with one-variable directional\ncalculations.\n\nDifferentiate the composition of  with the affine coordinate line.\nThe line has derivative equal to the th Euclidean basis vector and passes\nthrough  at parameter , so the chain rule gives exactly the\nFréchet derivative of  applied to that basis vector.\n\nLinearity of  is inherited from linearity of the Fréchet\nderivative.\n\nCoordinate differentiation obeys the usual linearity identities:and hence  and\n. The sum and difference\nidentities assume that  and  are differentiable at the point;\nconstant scaling and negation hold for the totalized derivative with no\nhypothesis.\n\nUnfold partialDeriv as the total Fréchet derivative evaluated on a basis\nvector. Apply Mathlib's derivative rules for addition, subtraction, negation,\nand constant scalar multiplication, then evaluate the resulting continuous\nlinear maps on that vector. The sum and difference rules use the stated\ndifferentiability hypotheses.\n\nThe iterated version has the same shape; for sums, differentiability must hold\nat every stage of the word.\n\nFor a derivative word  and fields\n,These two identities are unconditional because the total Fréchet derivative\ncommutes with zero and constant scalar multiplication even at\nnondifferentiability points. If  and  are globally , where\n is the length of , thenAll four statements allow an arbitrary real normed-space codomain .\n\nInduct on . The empty word reduces to the corresponding pointwise\nalgebra identity. At a successor word, unfold the outer coordinate\nderivative. Zero and scalar multiplication use their unconditional\nsingle-derivative rules. For addition and subtraction, global \nregularity makes every derivative along a proper tail of the word\ndifferentiable, so the\nsingle-coordinate sum or difference rule applies at the next step.\n\nCommuting two different coordinates is the first place where second-order\nregularity is essential.\n\nFor a  field  with arbitrary real normed-space\ncodomain, mixed coordinate derivatives commute:The pointwise theorem requires only ContDiffAt ℝ 2 f x; its global\nfunction-valued form assumes ContDiff ℝ 2 f. For a globally \nfield we also have the finite-block law\n\nThe  hypothesis gives symmetry of the second Fréchet derivative.\nEvaluate that symmetric bilinear map on the two coordinate basis vectors,\nidentify the evaluations with the two iterated partial derivatives, and use\nfunction extensionality for the global form. For finite blocks, induct on one\nblock length, commute one outer derivative past the other block, and use the\nregularity-budget theorem to retain the smoothness needed by the induction\nhypothesis.\n\nThe vector operators are assembled from coordinate derivatives.\n\nThe Euclidean gradient is the vector characterized byMathlib constructs it as the Riesz representative of the totalized Fréchet\nderivative, so it carries the same regularity caveat as partialDeriv.\n\nDivergence is the trace of the Jacobian. Composed with the gradient it gives the\nLaplacian, so both definitions are made from the same partialDeriv.\n\nFor a vector field , divergence isDifferentiability of the component functions is supplied by the theorem using\nthe operator rather than hidden in the definition.\n\nThe Laplacian is defined directly as the sum of the second same-coordinate\nderivatives rather than as , so that it is a\ntotalized expression in its own right.\n\nThe Euclidean Laplacian is the coordinate trace of the second derivative:For scalar fields this gives the operator in the classical Laplace and Poisson\nequations of Evans Chapter 2. The same sum makes sense for vector fields and\ncomplex-valued functions, so we use one definition, PDE.laplacian, for all of\nthem. Mathlib's canonical Laplacian.laplacian instead traces the totalized second\nFréchet derivative. The two agree under local  regularity; for a nonsmooth\nfunction, their totalized expressions can differ.\n\nLocality of the Laplacian is inherited from the locality of fderiv. It is the\ngerm statement behind the radial computations, where a first-derivative\nidentity valid near  is differentiated again at .\n\nThe Laplacian depends only on the germ of a field with values in a real normed space. If\n throughout a neighborhood of , thenThe theorem is stated for the totalized coordinate Laplacian and has no\ndifferentiability hypothesis; its content is locality of the totalized Fréchet\nderivatives.\n\nUnfold the Laplacian as a finite sum of same-coordinate second derivatives.\nNeighborhood equality is preserved by the first coordinate derivative on a\npossibly smaller neighborhood, and locality of the second derivative gives\nequality of each summand at . Sum these coordinatewise equalities.\n\nThe first explicit computation is the squared norm. It is smooth everywhere,\nincluding the origin, so its identities hold with no hypothesis.\n\nFor every ,\n\n\nDifferentiate the squared norm using the inner-product formula to obtain\n. Differentiating once more gives  in\neach coordinate. The Laplacian is therefore the sum of  copies of ,\nnamely .\n\nComposing a one-variable profile with the squared norm gives the first radial\nchain rule for the Laplacian.\n\nLet . If\n is  at , thenIn Lean,  and  are written deriv f and\nderiv (deriv f). This squared-radius formulation is valid at ;\nthere is no division by . It is therefore the preferred\nchain rule for Gaussian profiles, heat kernels, and other radial expressions\nthat are naturally smooth functions of .\n\nFor every coordinate , the first chain rule giveson a neighborhood of  where the local  hypothesis propagates.\nDifferentiate this product once more in coordinate :Sum over . The constant term contributes , while\n converts the second term to\n.\n\nComposing instead with the norm itself gives the classical radial formula away\nfrom the origin. The one-variable expression on the right is the\nradial Laplacian.\n\nLet  and let  be  at\n. Then the Laplacian of the radial field\n isThe formal coefficient in Lean is\n. The nonzero hypothesis is essential: the norm is not\nFréchet differentiable at the origin, and the displayed formula contains\n. At the origin one should instead use a theorem tailored to the\nprofile's parity or rewrite the field through the squared radius when\npossible.\n\nAway from zero, the derivative of the norm is the linear functional\n, henceUse neighborhood locality so that this first-derivative identity may be\ndifferentiated at . The product and quotient rules giveAfter summing, use . The second sum becomes\n, the third becomes , and the first consists of\n copies of ; collecting terms yields the stated\n.\n\nThe same computation for an inverse power of the distance to a fixed point\nproduces the kernels of potential theory.\n\nLet ,  and . Then\n\nWrite . Away from the pole,\n, and differentiating again in the\nsame coordinate givesSumming over  and using  gives\n. The derivative formulas come from a\nclosed-form Fréchet derivative of ,\nvalid on the open set .\n\nFor  the choice  makes the right side zero, so\n is harmonic away from . This is the Newtonian\nkernel behind the fundamental solution of Laplace's equation. The choice\n gives , which\nis used in the computations with Poisson kernels.\n\nThe radial formulas rest on a symmetry that the coordinate definition hides.\nThe sum  is written in one basis, yet it does not depend on\nwhich orthonormal basis is chosen, so rotations, reflections and translations\nof the argument commute with . This is why a rotation-invariant\nequation such as  has radial solutions to look for in the first\nplace.\n\nLet  be a linear isometry of , let , and\nlet  be  at . ThenFor Mathlib's basis-free Laplacian on finite-dimensional inner product\nspaces the identity  holds for any\nlinear isometric equivalence and needs no regularity.\n\nMathlib's Laplacian is the trace of the second derivative, and that trace\ncan be computed in any orthonormal basis. Computing\n in the standard basis  is the same as computing\n at  in the orthonormal basis . Where  is\n, the coordinate Laplacian agrees with Mathlib's, which gives the\nlinear case, and the translation  does not change second\nderivatives.\n\nProducts obey the Leibniz rule with one cross term, which is the gradient\npairing counted twice.\n\nIf  and  are  at , thenand each mixed second derivative satisfies\n at .\n\nApply the product rule to  on a\nneighbourhood of , where both factors are differentiable, and\ndifferentiate once more in direction . Setting  and summing over\n collects the two cross terms into .\n\nReturning to the vector operators, the only input needed to compare the\nLaplacian with  is the\ncoordinate law for the gradient.\n\nThe coordinate Laplacian agrees with the divergence of the canonical\ngradient:Both sides use the same totalized Fréchet-calculus convention.\n\nBy definition, . The gradient\nagreement theorem identifies , so each summand\nbecomes . The resulting sum\n is the coordinate definition of . The\nidentity is purely algebraic in the totalized expressions; no\ndifferentiability hypothesis is used.\n\nMathlib defines the gradient as a Riesz representative, so its coordinate law is\na theorem rather than a definition.\n\nFor every coordinate ,This is an identity between totalized expressions, because both sides use the\nsame Fréchet derivative.\n\nWrite the th gradient coordinate as its inner product with the th\nEuclidean basis vector. Mathlib's defining gradient identity turns this into\nthe Fréchet derivative applied to that vector, which is the definition of\npartialDeriv.\n\nThe coordinate law is the case  of the defining property of the\ngradient.\n\nFor every scalar field and direction , Mathlib's totalized gradient pairing satisfies\n\nThis is an unconditional identity between totalized Lean expressions.\n\nMathlib defines the Euclidean gradient as the Riesz representative of the\nFréchet derivative. The Riesz representation identity evaluated at \nis precisely .\n\nThe coordinate expansion of the Fréchet derivative is the common lemma behind\nscalar advection and vector-valued transport.\n\nFor a field  with arbitrary real normed-space codomain,\nthe Fréchet derivative expands in the Euclidean basis as\n\nThe scalar specialization rewrites scalar multiplication as ordinary real\nmultiplication. Both identities hold for the totalized derivative.\n\nExpand  in the standard Euclidean basis and use linearity of\n. Each basis-vector evaluation is the corresponding partialDeriv,\nwhich gives the vector-valued sum. In the scalar case, rewrite real scalar\nmultiplication as ordinary multiplication and commute the factors.\n\nThe advective derivative is the Fréchet derivative evaluated along the velocity\nfield itself. Defining it this way, rather than through the coordinate formula,\nkeeps scalar and vector-valued transport under one definition.\n\nFor a velocity field  and a field  with arbitrary normed-space\ncodomain, the advective derivative is\n\n\nFor a scalar field, the Fréchet definition expands exactly to the familiar\ncoordinate expression\n\nThis equality is algebraic in the totalized Fréchet derivative.\n\nUnfold advection as  and apply the scalar coordinate expansion\nof the Fréchet derivative with direction . Commutativity of real\nmultiplication puts each summand in the conventional order\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009PDE Problems and Differential Operators\u0009Coordinate derivatives and vector operators","header":"3.2.2.2. Conventions","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--Coordinate-derivatives-and-vector-operators--Conventions"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Riccati-comparison-model":{"contents":"For  with initial value , the explicit candidate isThe formula is total as a Lean function, including at its pole. Analytic\nstatements therefore work on  under .Evans notes the scalar model  before beginning the weighted\neigenfunction argument. The theorem carries a coefficient , an arbitrary\ninitial time, and the horizon formula.\n\nThe formula is only a candidate until its derivative has been computed. The\nrelevant hypothesis is the sign of the product , not separate signs of\n and .\n\nIf  and , the explicit formula satisfies  at\n.\n\nThe denominator factors as\n. Positivity of  and the inequality\n make both factors nonzero. The quotient rule can therefore be applied\nto the explicit formula, and a ring calculation reduces the derivative to\n.\n\nSolving the equation before the horizon is half of the comparison model; the\nother half identifies the limit at the horizon. For divergence to\n, rather than , we assume  and .\n\nIf  and , then  tends to  as  approaches\n from below. The corollary states this as\nNormBlowsUpAt (Riccati.solution a y₀ t₀) (Riccati.horizon a y₀ t₀).\n\nRewrite the solution as . As , the positive\nquantity  tends to zero from the right, so its reciprocal tends to\n; multiplication by  preserves that limit. Positivity of\nthe trajectory identifies order divergence with norm divergence.\n\nWhenever , the solution has the reciprocal-distance formThis algebraic identity is total in ; its analytic use is on the\npre-blow-up interval.\n\nSubstitute the definition of  and factor the original denominator as\n. Since , also ; cancelling this factor\nfrom the numerator and denominator yields . Lean's total\ndivision makes the resulting identity valid even at .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity\u0009Finite-Time Singularity Interfaces","header":"6.7.1.5.4. Riccati comparison model","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Riccati-comparison-model"}});